{
  "dom_id": "step-q250404",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The inverse of the function $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$",
